      <div class="bar-row"><span class="bl">Apple, medium (~182g)</span><div class="bb u-w-45-pct u-bg-green-200"><span class="bv u-text-hex-04342c">4.5 g</span></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><span class="bl">Broccoli, 100g</span><div class="bb u-w-26-pct u-bg-green-100"><span class="bv text-green-900">2.6 g</span></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><span class="bl">Wholegrain bread, 1 slice</span><div class="bb u-w-20-pct u-bg-green-75"><span class="bv text-green-900">2 g</span></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><span class="bl">White bread, 1 slice</span><div class="bb u-w-6-pct u-bg-hex-e1f5ee u-border-0-5px-solid-hex-9fe1cb"><span class="bv text-green-900">0.6 g</span></div></div>
      <p class="u-fs-11px text-secondary panel-note">Rule of thumb: ≥5g fibre per serving = "high fibre." ≥3g = "source of fibre" (EU labelling). Legumes beat everything else by a wide margin.</p>
